Evaluate the integral \( \int_0^{\pi/2} f(x) \, dx \), where the function \( f(x) \) is defined as follows:
\[
f(x) = \begin{cases} 
\sin(x), & \text{if } \cos(x) \in \mathbb{Q} \\
\sin^2(x), & \text{if } \cos(x) \notin \mathbb{Q}
\end{cases}
\]

results.final_visible:
Yes, such elements exist. In infinite multiplicative groups, elements can have infinite order. For example, in the multiplicative group of non-zero real numbers, the element $ 2 $ satisfies $ 2^n \neq 1 $ for all natural numbers $ n $. This is because $ 2^n $ grows without bound and never equals the identity element $ 1 $. Thus, the answer is $\boxed{\text{Yes}}$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{\text{Yes}}

