The matrix $ Q $ has orthonormal columns, so $ Q^T Q = I_n $, where $ I_n $ is the $ n \times n $ identity matrix. However, $ QQ^T $ is an $ m \times m $ matrix. Since $ m > n $, the column space of $ Q $ is a subspace of $ \mathbb{R}^m $ with dimension $ n $, and $ QQ^T $ projects vectors onto this subspace. The identity matrix $ I $ projects onto the entire space $ \mathbb{R}^m $, which is not possible here. Additionally, the rank of $ QQ^T $ is $ n $, while $ I $ has rank $ m $, so they cannot be equal.

